---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { rankedCount, sources, states } from "../lib/data";
import { sourceNote } from "../lib/format";

const boundarySources = sources.filter((s) => s.role === "boundaries");
const calibrationCount = sources.filter((s) => s.role === "calibration").length;
const redrawn = states.filter((s) => s.changed_since_2024).map((s) => s.name);
---

<Base title="How scores work | Gerrymandering Map" path="/methodology" description="How this site measures the shape of every congressional district, and what the scores can and can't tell you.">
  <main>
    <h1>How scores work</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      This site measures one thing: how compact each congressional district's shape is. A
      compact district is roughly round or square. An irregular one snakes, sprawls or has
      pieces cut out of it.
    </p>

    <h2>The three measures</h2>
    <p>Each runs from 0 to 1, where 1 is as compact as a shape can be.</p>
    <dl>
      <dt>Polsby-Popper</dt>
      <dd>
        Compares a district's area with its perimeter. A circle scores 1. Jagged edges and long
        thin arms add perimeter without adding area, so they pull the score down.
      </dd>
      <dt>Reock</dt>
      <dd>
        Compares a district's area with the smallest circle that fits around it. A stretched-out
        district fills little of its circle and scores low.
      </dd>
      <dt>Convex hull</dt>
      <dd>
        Compares a district's area with a rubber band stretched around it (its "convex hull"). A
        district with bites taken out of it, or tentacles reaching out, scores low.
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <h2>Measuring against land</h2>
    <p>
      Reock and convex hull count only the part of the circle or rubber band that falls on the
      district's own state. Water between islands or past a coastline is space no district could
      use, so it doesn't count against a district. Land a district leaves out still does.
      Without this, island districts such as Hawaii's 2nd or the Florida Keys would rank among
      the least compact for their geography alone.
    </p>
    <p>
      Polsby-Popper has no such adjustment, so a ragged coastline still lowers it. Each district
      is measured in an equal-area map projection centered on the district, so a district in
      Alaska is measured the same way as one in Maine.
    </p>

    <h2>From three measures to one score</h2>
    <p>
      For each measure, a district gets a percentile: where it falls among the {rankedCount}
      districts that have lines to draw. The six states with a single district covering the whole
      state are measured but not ranked. The <strong>irregularity score</strong> is 100 minus the
      average of the three percentiles, so 0 is the most compact district and scores near 100 are
      the least compact. Percentiles put the three measures on one scale, since their raw values
      sit in very different ranges.
    </p>
    <p>
      "Less compact than X% of districts" on each district's page comes straight from its rank
      by irregularity score.
    </p>

    <h2>What a score can't tell you</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Intent.</strong> An irregular shape is not proof of a gerrymander, and a compact
        one is not proof against it. A map of neat squares can still be drawn to favor one party.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Good reasons for odd shapes.</strong> Districts follow rivers, coastlines, county
        and city lines, and communities that belong together. The Voting Rights Act can require
        districts that keep a minority community together, and those can be irregular on purpose.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Comparisons with other sites.</strong> Measured perimeter depends on how detailed
        the map is, so these scores should only be compared with each other.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Which map</h2>
    <p>
      Scores are for the districts used in the November 3, 2026 election. Most come from the U.S.
      Census Bureau's map of the 2024 districts. {redrawn.length} states used new lines in 2026
      ({redrawn.join(", ")}), and for those the site uses each state's official 2026 map.
      Missouri and Virginia passed new maps that were not used in 2026, so they keep their earlier
      lines.
    </p>
    <p>
      The state files are much more detailed than the Census map, and more detail means a longer
      measured perimeter. So the new maps are simplified to the Census map's level of detail
      first. The settings were calibrated against the Census Bureau's own detailed and simplified
      versions of the same districts for 12 states, using {calibrationCount} extra Census files.
    </p>

    <h2>Data sources</h2>
    <ul class="sources">
      {
        boundarySources.map((s) => (
          <li>
            <a href={s.url}>{s.publisher}</a>: {sourceNote(s.description, s.license, s.downloaded)}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p>
      Every step, from download to score, is open source and can be rerun:
      {" "}<a href="https://github.com/rpivo/gerrymandering-map">github.com/rpivo/gerrymandering-map</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The code is under the MIT License. The scores, ranks and shapes this site produces are under
      {" "}<a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">Creative Commons Attribution 4.0</a>:
      you can reuse them for any purpose if you credit Gerrymandering Map.
    </p>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  main {
    max-width: 42rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 1.5rem 1rem;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 5vw, 2.5rem);
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  h2 {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    font-size: 1.25rem;
  }

  dt {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  dd {
    margin: 0.25rem 0 0;
  }

  li + li {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }

  .sources {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
</style>
